WEBVTT


00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.770
If you need more room at the hips and
tummy of your sweater when you shape the

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:10.990
side seams, you need to lengthen the
center front and back to keep them from

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:12.940
creeping up and being too short.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:19.750
You do this by working short rows at the
lower edge, adding length in the center

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:21.770
and not at the side seams.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:26.680
Let's say you want to add two inches
to the center back of your sweater.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:30.120
Your gauge is six rows to the inch.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.610
You'll want to add an additional
12 rows right here in the middle.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.840
Here's a generic outline.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:50.290
Start by casting on the required number of
stitches and work your ribbing or hem, or

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:52.120
however you're going
to finish your garment.

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:55.690
Then set your carriage to hold.

00:00:57.015 --> 00:01:02.474
Pull a few stitches opposite the carriage
to hold position knit across and wrap.

00:01:03.765 --> 00:01:07.425
Pull the same number of stitches
opposite the carriage to hold.

00:01:08.125 --> 00:01:09.835
Knit across and rap.

00:01:11.195 --> 00:01:14.714
Two rows have been knitted,
but none at the side seams.

00:01:17.345 --> 00:01:20.795
Next, pull a few more stitches
to hold opposite the carriage.

00:01:20.975 --> 00:01:22.685
Knit across and wrap.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:25.430
Do it again on the other side.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:30.090
A few more stitches to hold opposite
the carriage knit across, and wrap.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.290
A total of four rows have
been knitted in the center.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:40.030
Now repeat this for the total of the 12
rows tha you need for your two inches.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.420
On the last row,

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:49.300
wrap for the last time and then put the
needles opposite the carriage into working

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:49.870
position.

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:51.580
Cancel.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:52.030
Hold.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.130
Reset your row counter and
continue following your pattern.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:01.420
Notice that I left about a third of the
stitches in work at the center back.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.930
You don't want to short row
to a point in the middle.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:10.940
Now, if you want to be more precise,
you could do the math and work this out

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:11.690
perfectly.

00:02:13.755 --> 00:02:15.765
I hope this has been
a helpful tip for you.

00:02:16.605 --> 00:02:21.855
If you aren't familiar with working short
rows, they are a very powerful tool in

00:02:21.855 --> 00:02:23.115
your knitting toolbox.

00:02:24.195 --> 00:02:29.205
The learning library has quite a few
tutorials on using short rows, both

00:02:29.205 --> 00:02:32.115
decorative uses and
practical ones like this.

